 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** The server broadcasts committed revisions over persistent WebSocket connections managed in-process. HTTPS polling was rejected because a few seconds of lag at opening-time burst rates is worse than the extra connection management work. A hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring vendor fees and provider coupling, which the coordinator-scale load (800 browsers, 20 edits/second peak) does not require. WebSockets obligate the change store to maintain a publication outbox and support cursor queries, the delivery layer to manage sessions and replay, and the browser to implement reconnect and catch-up logic.
